course: dictionary
lesson: 12636
title: Disparaging: a quiet insult
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you what someone thinks of a person or thing, even when they do not say it outright. «Disparaging» is one of those words. It means showing that you think someone or something is not good or not important.
explain: A disparaging remark is often quiet. It is not a shout. It is a small, sly put-down. «The coach made disparaging remarks about our team, and the other players laughed.» The remarks were insulting, even though they were said quietly.
explain: Look for clues. Words that say something is not good, or a sneering tone, show that a comment is disparaging. Ask yourself: is this person trying to make someone else seem small? If the answer is yes, the word you need is probably disparaging.
fact: Disparaging means showing that you think someone or something is not good or not important.
fact: A disparaging remark is an insult said in a quiet way.
fact: «Disparaging» can go with remarks, comments, or a look: «a disparaging look».
fact: Disparaging comments can hurt feelings, and they can be a form of bullying.
fact: In the news, «disparaging remarks» about a group of people are often reported and criticised.
exercise: «Her disparaging remarks about my drawing made me want to stop.» What does disparaging mean? | Showing that she is very tired | Showing that she has never seen a drawing | Showing that she loves my drawing very much | *Showing that she thinks my drawing is not good
exercise: Which phrase is a disparaging comment? | A kind compliment about someone's work | A song sung at a birthday party | *A remark that says someone's work is not important | A question about the time
exercise: Ben says, «Their comments were disparaging, so I felt small.» Why did Ben feel small? | They forgot to say hello | They asked him to sing a song | *They said things that showed they thought he was not good enough | They gave him a present
test: «The critic's disparaging review made the play sound boring and worthless.» What does disparaging show? | *That the critic thought the play was not good | That the critic loved the play | That the critic was asleep during the play | That the play was very long
test: Which is the best meaning of disparaging? | Very loud and happy | *Showing you think someone is not important | Full of bright colours | Kind and helpful
exam: «Stop making disparaging comments about Sam's cake, Priya.» Why should Priya stop? | *Disparaging comments are insulting and can hurt Sam's feelings | Cakes are unhealthy to eat | Sam has not finished baking the cake | Priya is not allowed to talk at all
